# Rank 1675 / CVE-2024-49761 (GHSA-2rxp-v6pw-ch6m): REXML's base parser
# unescapes entity references in parsed XML with a static regex whose `0*`
# prefix and whose first alternation branch `\\d+` can both consume the same
# run of `0` characters, so the engine must retry every split of that run --
# a ReDoS quadratic in the number of digits between `&#` and `x...;`
# (CWE-1333 inefficient regular expression complexity, CWE-400).
#
# `Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES` (lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb:153 at the
# fix parent) is `/&#0*((?:\\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/`, and
# `BaseParser#unnormalize` applies it to the string it is handed with a
# global `rv.gsub!(Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES) { ... }` (baseparser.rb:571).
# The string is parsed XML content, which is the input an XML parser exists to
# accept, so the cost is entirely the attacker's; the advisory's trigger is
# "many digits between &# and x...; in a hex numeric character reference", and
# only Ruby 3.1's regexp engine is affected. The fixed revision removes the `0*`
# prefix entirely: leading zeros before `x` are invalid XML character references
# anyway (the added regression test asserts `&#x61;&#0x61;` unescapes to the
# literal text `a&#0x61;`), and a digit run then has exactly one consumer --
# `\\d+` alone -- instead of two overlapping ones.
#
# The pool row is correct in every field: ce59f2eb1aeb is a real, readable,
# single-library-file commit plus its regression test ("parser: fix a bug that
# &#0x...; is accepted as a character reference"), the vulnerable regex with
# the `0*` prefix is present at its parent, the remediation (no `0*`, and the
# simplified block that parses the `x` prefix explicitly) is present at the
# commit itself, and the v3.3.9 release tag contains it.
#
# FIXED, 2nd pass. The 1st pass pinned an engine gap: the run-split's second
# consumer sat inside an unquantified alternation group, invisible to
# `regexSplitsOneCharRun`'s sibling-only scan (regexambig.go), so
# `rubyRegexMayBacktrack` answered false and the literal lowered to a bare
# `nir.Const` no binding could label. The classifier now descends into
# unquantified group heads for the second consumer of a single-character run,
# and the ruby frontend stamps each static literal the analysis flags as
# `analysis.dos.catastrophic_regex` at the literal itself, which
# `catastrophicRegex1704` (vyql/bindings/ruby/m5a48bf47.vyql) reports through
# VYQL-DOS-003 -- the same definition-site route the java.util.regex and re.*
# arms already take. Measured on the real checkout (`--cache off`, bounded):
# 105 findings at both revisions before, 109 at the vulnerable and 108 at the
# fixed tree after; the single discriminating row is the CVE's own literal at
# baseparser.rb:153. The three rows the change adds at text.rb (:24
# NUMERICENTITY, :379's inline gsub, :44 VALID_XML_CHARS) fire at both
# revisions: ce59f2eb patched baseparser only, so text.rb still carries the
# same `&#0*...` run-split today -- an upstream residual of this very
# weakness, correctly still reported.
#
# The taint route (VYQL-DOS-002) stays closed for the hoisted-constant shape --
# a regex supplied as a call argument lowers to an `__regex.match` node with no
# link to the receiver it is applied to, the gap rank 1648 recorded -- so the
# definition-site review is the route that answers this spelling. In the
# `case value when /re/` shape, whose operand link the engine does build, the
# newly classified spelling now reports DOS-002 as well, which is the third
# block below: the two spellings of the run-split are one construct, and the
# pair that discriminates is the `0*` prefix against its removal.
#
# verify_current_expected: fixed-engine-classifier, definition-site review.

test "rank1675: the character-reference regex splits a zero run between the 0* prefix and the nested digit branch"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-003
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  module REXML
    module Parsers
      class BaseParser
        module Private
          CHARACTER_REFERENCES = /&#0*((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        end
        private_constant :Private

        def unnormalize( string, entities=nil, filter=nil )
          rv = string.dup
          matches = rv.scan( REFERENCE_RE )
          return rv if matches.size == 0
          rv.gsub!( Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES ) {
            m=$1
            m = "0#{m}" if m[0] == ?x
            [Integer(m)].pack('U*')
          }
          rv
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675: the fixed regex leaves one consumer for a digit run and the block parses the x prefix explicitly"
  lang ruby
  file lib/rexml/parsers/baseparser.rb
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-001
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  module REXML
    module Parsers
      class BaseParser
        module Private
          CHARACTER_REFERENCES = /&#((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        end
        private_constant :Private

        def unnormalize( string, entities=nil, filter=nil )
          rv = string.dup
          matches = rv.scan( REFERENCE_RE )
          return rv if matches.size == 0
          rv.gsub!( Private::CHARACTER_REFERENCES ) {
            m=$1
            if m.start_with?("x")
              code_point = Integer(m[1..-1], 16)
            else
              code_point = Integer(m, 10)
            end
            [code_point].pack('U*')
          }
          rv
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the same run-split with the second consumer as a sibling single-char repeat is reported"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  class CharRef
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /&#0*0+;/
        "a"
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the CVE's spelling, the digit class inside the unquantified alternation group, is reported the same way"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  expect VYQL-DOS-002
  code
  ```
  class CharRef
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /&#0*((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        "a"
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1675 pair: the fixed spelling keeps the alternation and drops the 0* prefix"
  lang ruby
  profile library
  reject VYQL-DOS-002
  reject VYQL-DOS-003
  code
  ```
  class CharRef
    def call(value)
      case value
      when /&#((?:\d+)|(?:x[a-fA-F0-9]+));/
        "a"
      else
        value
      end
    end
  end
  ```
